    "body": "Blue Bird plans to invest approximately $300 million in a school bus factory in Fort Valley, Georgia, creating 375 jobs over three years, according to an announcement from Gov. Brian Kemp.\nWhat’s Related\nThe company will convert a former diesel-powered motorhome manufacturing site into a facility of about 1 million square feet. Construction is expected to begin in the first quarter of 2027.\nThe factory will produce Type C school buses, including diesel, gasoline, propane, and electric models. Planned hiring includes manufacturing, plant operations and technician positions.\nThe state announcement did not give an opening date, but Blue Bird’s May 2026 investor presentation identified the fourth quarter of 2028 as the anticipated production start for its new Fort Valley plant.\nFactory plans include automation\nBlue Bird’s earlier presentation described a factory designed to produce 9,000 Type C buses, with automation to begin when production starts. The company said separating products and adding automation would help improve manufacturing efficiency.\nThat presentation put the total investment at more than $300 million and identified $80 million in funding from the U.S. Department of Energy’s Office of Manufacturing and Energy Supply Chains. The October announcement did not update the status of that funding or disclose a dollar amount for state or local incentives.\nThe latest announcement places the project at an existing industrial property previously used to manufacture motorhomes. It also confirms that the planned school bus production will cover four fuel and power options, rather than electric buses alone.\nPride of Middle Georgia\nBlue Bird already has more than 1,900 employees in Georgia, according to the state. The company expects to add 375 positions over three years, although the announcement did not break down hiring by year or job category.\n“Every bus that rolls out of our plant carries the pride of the people across Middle Georgia who build it,” said John Wyskiel, Blue Bird’s president and CEO. The company directs applicants to its careers website for information about available positions.\nThe Georgia Department of Economic Development worked with the Development Authority of Peach County and Georgia Quick Start on the project. Fort Valley Mayor Shakeena B. Reeves pointed to the city’s longstanding relationship with the manufacturer.\n“For generations, Fort Valley has helped Blue Bird move America forward,” Reeves said.",
